1
00:00:01,260 --> 00:00:02,240
Hi again.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,880
In the previous video, you
learned about the value of data.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:10,138
In this lesson, we'll discuss the common
types of data that project managers

4
00:00:10,138 --> 00:00:12,010
collect and analyze regularly.

5
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:17,490
We'll also discuss the tools you'll need
to organize this data for your projects.

6
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:18,950
Ready to get started?

7
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:19,910
Okay, let's go!

8
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:25,485
As we discussed earlier, data is the
collection of facts that can help inform

9
00:00:25,485 --> 00:00:29,999
decisions. You may start to notice how
the project data impacts the team's daily

10
00:00:29,999 --> 00:00:33,940
activities and the overall progress and
the success of the project.

11
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:38,210
This is done by using
various types of metrics.

12
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:42,279
A metric is a quantifiable
measurement that is used to track and

13
00:00:42,279 --> 00:00:44,287
assess a business objective.

14
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:46,870
Metrics are based on selected goals.

15
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:51,148
They vary per project and
serve as a key type of project data.

16
00:00:51,148 --> 00:00:55,001
There are many types of project data
you can use to determine progress and

17
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:56,330
efficiency.

18
00:00:56,330 --> 00:01:00,550
You can group project metrics into
productivity metrics and quality metrics.

19
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:06,310
Productivity typically measures
progress and output over time.

20
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:10,060
Productivity metrics allow you
to track the effectiveness and

21
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:14,657
efficiency of your project and
include metrics like milestones, tasks,

22
00:01:14,657 --> 00:01:17,420
projections, and duration.

23
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,360
Let's break down each metric.

24
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:23,392
As you may recall from an earlier course,
a milestone is an important point

25
00:01:23,392 --> 00:01:26,592
within the project schedule
that indicates progress and

26
00:01:26,592 --> 00:01:31,330
usually signifies completion of a
deliverable or phase of the project.

27
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:33,868
And a project task is
an activity that needs to be

28
00:01:33,868 --> 00:01:36,950
accomplished within a set period of time.

29
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:42,880
A projection is how you predict an outcome
based on the information you have now.

30
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:47,168
For example, you can predict that with
the resources you have at the start of

31
00:01:47,168 --> 00:01:50,520
the project, the project will
be completed in six months.

32
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:55,538
The duration of a project is the total
time it takes to complete a project from

33
00:01:55,538 --> 00:01:57,220
start to finish.

34
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:59,527
Duration can also be used for
milestones and

35
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:02,020
determining if you'll
meet project deadlines.

36
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:09,280
This data is broken down into hours,
days, weeks, months, and sometimes years.

37
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:12,871
So to recap,
productivity metrics include milestones,

38
00:02:12,871 --> 00:02:15,300
tasks, projections, and duration.

39
00:02:17,330 --> 00:02:19,355
Now let's discuss quality metrics.

40
00:02:19,355 --> 00:02:22,945
Quality metrics relates to
achieving acceptable outcomes and

41
00:02:22,945 --> 00:02:25,855
can include metrics such
as the number of changes,

42
00:02:25,855 --> 00:02:29,120
issues, and cost variance,
which all affect quality.

43
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,230
Let's discuss each metric.

44
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:37,330
The number of changes during the project
or project scope helps to monitor risks.

45
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:42,160
Changes show any inconsistencies from
the initial requirements of the project.

46
00:02:43,190 --> 00:02:47,591
A series of compounding small changes
may indicate a bigger issue and

47
00:02:47,591 --> 00:02:50,463
provide early warning
signs of these issues.

48
00:02:50,463 --> 00:02:54,363
Using a change log to keep a record
of these changes is a useful tool for

49
00:02:54,363 --> 00:02:58,736
communicating with stakeholders about
why something is taking a long time or

50
00:02:58,736 --> 00:03:01,190
costs more than expected.

51
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:06,140
A change log is a record of all of
the notable changes on a project.

52
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:11,520
An issue is a known and real problem that
may affect the ability to complete a task.

53
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:15,067
For example, an issue might be
the delay of legal approval for

54
00:03:15,067 --> 00:03:17,797
an advertisement you
were hoping to launch or

55
00:03:17,797 --> 00:03:23,010
the missing number of confirmed seats to
secure a venue for a business conference.

56
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:25,970
Another quality metric is cost variance.

57
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:30,140
It illustrates the difference between
the actual cost and the budget cost.

58
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:30,830
Simply put,

59
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:35,580
cost variance compares what you plan to
spend versus what you actually spent.

60
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:37,500
Let's put it in an example.

61
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:43,312
If you budgeted to only host 250 attendees
at an upcoming business conference but

62
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:48,980
275 people showed up and the venue
charges you for those additional guests,

63
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:53,403
the actual costs will be higher than
your initial budget or estimate.

64
00:03:53,403 --> 00:03:59,003
So to recap, quality metrics can
include things like number of changes,

65
00:03:59,003 --> 00:04:01,272
issues, and cost variance.

66
00:04:01,272 --> 00:04:05,268
While these data points are commonly
tracked by project managers, there

67
00:04:05,268 --> 00:04:09,341
are dozens more that you can leverage
to inform decisions in your project.

68
00:04:09,341 --> 00:04:13,153
The great news is that there's
a lot of sophisticated software and

69
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:17,110
tools dedicated to project management and
data analysis,

70
00:04:17,110 --> 00:04:21,136
so you'll have help keeping track of all
of these different types of data in one

71
00:04:21,136 --> 00:04:22,820
centralized location.

72
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:27,138
Project management tools like
Workfront and JIRA track activity and

73
00:04:27,138 --> 00:04:28,930
provide readable results

74
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:32,241
so you can measure the overall
health of your project.

75
00:04:32,241 --> 00:04:36,700
Data analysis tools like Tableau
are useful for data visualization.

76
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:41,060
Now you know a bit more about how
to identify common types of data.

77
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:43,866
You can also ID common project
management software and

78
00:04:43,866 --> 00:04:46,370
tools that can help you manage and
analyze data.

79
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:51,580
In the next lesson, we'll cover how data
helps you to make informed decisions.

80
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:52,390
Meet you there.
